Six Types of Cloud Deployment Models Available:
    Public (Most Common)
        - System and users interact with devices on public networks, such as the internet and other clouds
        - Ex. Google Drive
        - Provides organizations a quick and cost-efficient way to access essential services

    Private
        - Used by different organizations within the cloud
        - Chosen when security is more important than having a lower cost
        - Ex. GovCloud
    Hybrid
        - A mix of public and private, where you are able to keep sensitive data private and still be able to handle traffic spikes with certain data being public
    Community
        - Infrastructure is shared between several different orgs with common service needs
        - Like compliance rules or security requirements
        - Data is seperated to each org, but go through a funnel of rules and requirements first.
    Multi-Tenancy
        - Allows customers to share computing resources in a public or private cloud
        - Secuirty concerns
        - One customer gets a DoS attack, the entire server goes through the same attack. All the connecting customers to that server are affected as well
    Single-Tenancy
        - Assigns a particular resource to a customer
        - Less efficient, and more expensive